CAT = "cusGrammar"; SEG = "GRM"
Q = [
 ("Which sentence uses \"such ... that\" correctly?",
 "It was such a heavy load that the crane failed.", ["It was so a heavy load that the crane failed.", "It was such heavy load that the crane failed.", "It was such a heavy load as the crane failed."],
 "\"Such\" comes before \"a\" + adjective + noun (\"such a heavy load\") and is followed by \"that\". \"So\" goes before an adjective on its own (\"so heavy\")."),
 ("Which sentence uses \"lend\" or \"borrow\" correctly?",
 "Could you lend me your torch?", ["Could you borrow me your torch?", "Could you lent me your torch?", "Could you lend to me your torch?"],
 "You lend something to someone and borrow something from someone. \"Borrow me\" is a common error for \"lend me\"."),
 ('The officer insisted that the driver ___ the lorry for inspection. Which option fills the gap?', 'open', ['opening', 'to open', 'will open'], 'After a verb of demand such as insist, formal English uses the base form (the subjunctive): "that the driver open". "Should open" would also be correct. "To open" and "opening" cannot follow "that the driver", and "will" does not fit the past "insisted".'),
 ("The agents ___ at the gate since six o'clock this morning, and they are still there. Which option fills the gap?",
 "have been waiting", ["had been waiting", "are waiting", "were waiting"],
 "An action that started in the past and is still going on now takes the present perfect continuous, \"have been waiting\", with \"since\" + a point in time."),
 ('The consignment was cleared ___ the agent had paid the duty. Which option fills the gap?', 'after', ['despite', 'unless', 'whereas'], '"After" shows the order of events: payment first, clearance second. "Despite" would need a noun phrase and suggests a contrast that is not there.'),
 ('Which sentence follows the rule for "one of the officers who"?', 'She is one of the officers who were trained.', ['She is one of the officers who was trained.', 'She is one of the officers who is trained.', 'She is one of the officers who has trained.'], 'The relative pronoun "who" refers to "officers", which is plural, so its verb is plural. Many writers wrongly match it to "one".'),
 ('The new scanner is ___ than the one at the old terminal. Which option fills the gap?', 'more reliable', ['more reliabler', 'most reliable', 'reliabler'], 'Long adjectives form the comparative with "more". "Most reliable" is the superlative, used for three or more things.'),
 ("___ the agent declared the goods, they would not have been seized. Which option fills the gap?",
 "Had", ["If", "Has", "Did"],
 "\"Had the agent declared\" is the inverted form of \"If the agent had declared\". \"If the agent declared\" would not match \"would not have been seized\"."),
 ('The patrol team divided the work ___ its five members. Which option fills the gap?', 'among', ['between', 'within', 'across'], '"Among" is used for more than two people; "between" is the traditional choice for two.'),
 ('Which sentence uses the word "information" correctly?', 'The information was useful.', ['The informations were useful.', 'The information were useful.', 'An information was useful.'], '"Information" is uncountable: it has no plural form, takes a singular verb and is never used with "an".'),
 ("Which sentence puts \"only\" in the right place to say that nobody else checked the manifest?",
 "Only Musa checked the manifest.", ["Musa only checked the manifest.", "Musa checked only the manifest.", "Musa checked the only manifest."],
 "\"Only\" belongs next to the word it limits. To say no one but Musa checked it, put \"only\" before \"Musa\". \"Checked only the manifest\" means he checked nothing else."),
 ('I would rather you ___ the forms before Friday. Which option fills the gap?', 'submitted', ['submit', 'will submit', 'had submit'], 'After "would rather" with a different subject, English uses the past form for a present or future wish: "I would rather you submitted".'),
 ('Which sentence uses "object to" correctly?', 'She objected to paying the fee.', ['She objected to pay the fee.', 'She objected paying the fee.', 'She objected for paying the fee.'], 'In "object to", "to" is a preposition, so it is followed by an -ing form: "objected to paying". The verb "object" also needs "to"; it cannot take "for" or no preposition.'),
 ('Which sentence with "unless" is correct?', 'Unless you declare it, it will be seized.', ["Unless you don't declare it, it will be seized.", 'Unless you will declare it, it will be seized.', 'Unless you declared it, it will be seized.'], '"Unless" already means "if not", so adding "don\'t" reverses the meaning. In a first conditional the unless-clause takes the present tense, not "will".'),
 ("The seized goods ___ to the federal warehouse tomorrow. Which option fills the gap?",
 "will be taken", ["will take", "will be took", "are taking"],
 "The goods do not take themselves, so the passive is needed, and \"tomorrow\" needs the future: \"will be taken\"."),
 ("The tanker collided ___ a bus near the border. Which option fills the gap?",
 "with", ["on", "against", "to"],
 "The verb \"collide\" is followed by \"with\": one vehicle collides with another."),
 ('Which sentence about the distance is correct?', 'Twenty kilometres is a long walk.', ['Twenty kilometres are a long walk.', 'Twenty kilometre is a long walk.', 'Twenty kilometre are a long walk.'], 'A distance treated as one amount takes a singular verb, and the noun stays plural after twenty: "twenty kilometres is".'),
 ('Which sentence ending with "shall we" or a similar tag is correct?', "Let's check the seals, shall we?", ["Let's check the seals, will we?", "Let's check the seals, don't we?", "Let's check the seals, shan't we?"], 'A suggestion beginning with "Let\'s" takes the tag "shall we?".'),
 ("The lorry was stopped ___ the checkpoint at Seme. Which option fills the gap?",
 "at", ["in", "on", "into"],
 "\"At\" is used for a particular point or place such as a checkpoint, gate or junction."),
 ('Which sentence uses the correct preposition after "comply"?', 'All agents must comply with the rules.', ['All agents must comply to the rules.', 'All agents must comply by the rules.', 'All agents must comply on the rules.'], '"Comply" takes "with". "Abide by" is the phrase that uses "by", which is why that option tempts some candidates.'),
 ('Which sentence is correctly punctuated?', "The officers' canteen is closed.", ["The officer's' canteen is closed.", "The officers canteen' is closed.", "The officers's canteen is closed."], "For a plural noun ending in s, the possessive apostrophe goes after the s: officers'. The other forms are not standard."),
 ('Which sentence beginning with "Not only" is correct?', 'Not only did he pay, he also apologised.', ['Not only he paid, he also apologised.', 'Not only he did pay, he also apologised.', 'Not only paid he, he also apologised.'], 'When a sentence opens with "Not only", the auxiliary comes before the subject: "Not only did he pay".'),
 ('The goods were seized because they were ___ on the manifest. Which option fills the gap?', 'not listed', ['not listing', 'no listed', 'unlisting'], 'The passive needs the past participle: "were not listed". "Not listing" would make the goods the ones doing the listing.'),
 ('Which spelling of the word meaning "to go beyond a set limit" is correct?', 'exceed', ['excede', 'exseed', 'exced'], 'Exceed is spelt with -ceed, like proceed and succeed. Most other words with that sound, such as concede and precede, end in -cede.'),
 ('Which spelling of the word meaning "close working contact between groups" is correct?', 'liaison', ['liason', 'liaision', 'laison'], 'Liaison has an i on both sides of the a: l-i-a-i-s-o-n. Dropping the second i is the most common error.'),
 ('The fewer the documents missing, the ___ the clearance. Which option fills the gap?', 'faster', ['fastest', 'more fast', 'fast'], 'The pattern "the fewer..., the ..." takes a comparative in both halves: "the faster".'),
 ('Which sentence uses the pronoun after "except" correctly?', 'Everyone signed the register except him.', ['Everyone signed the register except he.', 'Everyone signed the register except himself.', 'Everyone signed the register except his.'], '"Except" is a preposition here, so it takes the object pronoun "him". "Himself" is only used when it refers back to the same person as the subject.'),
 ("Which sentence uses \"which\" correctly to add extra information?",
 "The Apapa port, which is in Lagos, is busy.", ["The Apapa port, that is in Lagos, is busy.", "The Apapa port which, is in Lagos, is busy.", "The Apapa port, who is in Lagos, is busy."],
 "Extra information about a thing goes between commas and starts with \"which\". \"That\" is not used after a comma, and \"who\" is for people."),
 ('The officer was ___ duty when the lorry arrived at the gate. Which option fills the gap?', 'on', ['in', 'at', 'by'], 'The fixed phrase is "on duty", meaning working at the time. "Off duty" is its opposite.'),
 ("Which sentence uses \"ought to\" correctly?",
 "Travellers ought to declare all cash.", ["Travellers ought declare all cash.", "Travellers ought to declaring all cash.", "Travellers oughts to declare all cash."],
 "\"Ought\" is always followed by \"to\" and the base verb, and it never takes an -s: \"ought to declare\"."),
 ('The inspector wanted to "get to the bottom of" the matter. Which option is nearest in meaning to the phrase in quotes?', 'Find the real cause', ['Close the case quickly', 'Lower the duty charged', 'Report to a superior'], 'To get to the bottom of something is to discover its true cause or full facts.'),
 ('The forms must be filled ___ in block letters. Which option fills the gap?', 'in', ['up', 'on', 'at'], '"Fill in" a form is the standard British usage. "Fill up" is used for a container such as a tank.'),
]
